      {
        "id": 223857,
        "name": "Gus_Smedstad",
        "username": "Gus_Smedstad",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/g/e8c25b/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2018-11-01T00:55:35.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Kind of a sidebar, but...</p>\n<p>In almost all circumstances, I use Dark Conductor Argent Adept’s power on two hero decks. That’s choosing between two possible positive outcomes. Villain cards are always bad, and environment cards tend to be bad with rare positive outcomes. Giving either one an extra play rarely makes sense, so you’re effectively robbing yourself of choice by picking either one. If, say, 1 out of 6 environment cards is good, why take that chance vs. a 100% chance of a beneficial choice from a hero deck? A particular&nbsp;environment card has to be absolutely critical to your strategy for that to make sense.</p>\n<p>There are some corner cases, of course. There was a one-shot with Spite, Agent of Gloom where using Dark Conductor’s power was the only way to get enough victim cards in play to flip his drugs back. But it’s pretty unusual.</p>",
        "post_number": 21,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:57:07.121Z",
